WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.803
Gas law: what happens to cylinder pressure when the cylinder warms up or cools down?

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:17.033
Set a preset, fill pressure, size and two temperatures. The cylinder is rigid, so pressure simply follows temperature: Gay-Lussac’s law.

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:26.435
Example one: a 12-litre cylinder filled to 200 bar in a warm shop at 35 °C, then cooled in a lake to 4 °C.

00:00:26.585 --> 00:00:30.890
Work it out first, then click Show Result.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:37.267
The gauge reads about 180 bar: 20 bar gone, and nothing leaked.

00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:50.220
In the ratio, temperatures must be in kelvin: add 273.15. In °C, 4/35 would claim almost all the gas vanished.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.773
That is 241 l less gas at 1 bar, about 4 minutes less at 20 m.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:07.193
Example two: the same cylinder, 200 bar at 20 °C, left in the sun until it reaches 70 °C.

00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:15.445
Now it climbs to about 234 bar, above the 200 bar working pressure. Keep cylinders in the shade.

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:29.022
Caveat: the law needs absolute pressure, gauge + about 1 bar. The page uses the gauge reading directly, which shifts the result by only a fraction of a bar.

00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:34.024
Try it yourself in the Deco Theory sandbox. Thanks for watching!
